RCON architecture — final structure

The goal is:

RconSession is the single source of truth for one server/RCON connection.

It owns the connection, the live-log instance, all session state, and all operations belonging to that server.

The UI only consumes its state and calls its methods.

File structure
custom_components/rcon/
│
├── mod.rs
│
├── session.rs
│   └── RconSession
│       ├── state
│       ├── RconClient
│       ├── LiveLog instance
│       ├── connect()
│       ├── disconnect()
│       ├── execute()
│       ├── process_log()
│       ├── push_log()
│       ├── pause()
│       ├── unpause()
│       └── other session operations
│
├── connection.rs
│   └── low-level RCON connection/background handling
│
├── players.rs
│   └── RconPlayers + player parsing/updating
│
└── ui/
    ├── mod.rs
    ├── console.rs
    │   └── session selection + tabs + login UI
    │
    ├── overview.rs
    │   └── scoreboard + server controls
    │
    ├── chat.rs
    │   └── chat display
    │
    └── terminal.rs
        └── terminal + filters + command input

commands.rs is not necessarily needed anymore. If a function is fundamentally an operation on a session, it belongs in impl RconSession.

State ownership
AppState
│
└── rcon_sessions:
    Signal<HashMap<SocketAddr, RconSession>>
                            │
                            ▼
                     RconSession
                     ├── addr
                     ├── client
                     ├── live_log
                     ├── status
                     ├── logs
                     ├── players
                     └── match_paused

The outer signal answers:

Which sessions exist?

The state inside RconSession answers:

What is happening inside this session?

RconSession owns reactive state
RconSession
│
├── client
├── live_log
│
├── status        → Signal<RconStatus>
├── logs          → Signal<Vec<RconLogEvent>>
├── players       → Signal<RconPlayers>
└── match_paused  → Signal<bool>

The exact type of live_log depends on the existing live_log API, but conceptually it belongs to the session.

There is one live-log instance per server, so there is no reason to manage it separately from the session representing that server.

Session API

Everything that changes session state goes through RconSession.

RconSession
│
├── connect()
├── disconnect()
│
├── execute(command)
│
├── process_log(parsed_line)
├── push_log(event)
│
├── update_players(...)
│
├── pause()
└── unpause()

The UI does not do:

client.lock()
logs.write()
players.write()
paused.set(...)

Instead:

session.execute(...)
session.pause()
session.unpause()

This keeps the ownership in one place.

Live-log flow

The global HTTP listener is only transport infrastructure.

Game Server
    │
    ▼
HTTP listener
    │
    ▼
route by SocketAddr
    │
    ▼
corresponding RconSession
    │
    ▼
session.process_log(...)
    │
    ▼
session's live_log / parser handling
    │
    ▼
ParsedLine
    │
    ▼
RconSession updates its state

The important boundary is:

The HTTP layer delivers the log to the correct session. The session owns what happens with it.

Incoming game log
Game Server
    │
    ▼
HTTP listener
    │
    ▼
LogParser / live_log
    │
    ▼
ParsedLine
    │
    ▼
RconSession::process_log()
    │
    ├── logs.write()
    ├── players.write()       if relevant
    ├── score/state update    if relevant
    └── other state changes
            │
            ▼
          Dioxus
RCON command
UI
 │
 ▼
session.execute("mp_restartgame 1")
 │
 ├── RCON client
 │
 ├── logs.push("> mp_restartgame 1")
 │
 ├── logs.push(response)
 │
 └── update state if necessary
        │
        ▼
      Dioxus

The UI doesn't need to know how the command is executed or how the response gets into the log.

UI flow
RconConsole
│
├── finds RconSession by SocketAddr
│
├── Login / connection state
│
└── authenticated
      │
      ├── RconOverview
      │     ├── score
      │     ├── players
      │     ├── pause/unpause
      │     ├── restart
      │     └── RconChat
      │
      └── RconTerminal
            ├── logs
            ├── filters
            └── command input

Both Chat and Terminal consume the same session log signal:

                 RconSession
                      │
                logs: Signal<...>
                 /           \
                ▼             ▼
           RconChat       Terminal

Therefore:

session.logs.write().push(...)
              │
              ▼
       Signal changes
              │
        ┌─────┴─────┐
        ▼           ▼
      Chat       Terminal

No copying of the log Vec and no passing &mut Vec around.

The fundamental rule
AppState
   │
   │ manages which sessions exist
   ▼
RconSession
   │
   │ owns connection + live_log + state + behavior
   ▼
Reactive Signals
   │
   │ consumed by
   ▼
UI components

And the key ownership principle is now:

One server = one RconSession = one RCON client + one live-log instance + all state and behavior belonging to that server.

This is the version I'd use as our architectural reference going forward. If we deliberately change this structure later, we update this reference before continuing implementation.